<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">5 April in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">7 events that happened on 5 April, from 823 to 1994.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">823</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Paschal I crowns Lothar co-emperor in Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On Easter Sunday Pope Paschal I crowned Lothar, son of Louis the Pious, as co-emperor in St Peter's. The ceremony reinforced the idea that imperial legitimacy came through papal coronation in Rome.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1242</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Alexander Nevsky defeats the Teutonic Order on frozen Lake Peipus</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Novgorod's forces under Prince Alexander Nevsky beat the Livonian branch of the Teutonic Order in the Battle on the Ice, halting the Order's eastward expansion into Russian lands.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1722</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Jacob Roggeveen reaches Easter Island</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Dutch navigator Jacob Roggeveen became the first known European to reach Rapa Nui, naming it after the day of his arrival, Easter Sunday. His crew recorded the island's great stone moai.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1792</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">George Washington issues the first presidential veto</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Washington vetoed a bill apportioning seats in the House of Representatives, judging it unconstitutional. It was the first use of a power that later presidents would wield thousands of times.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1879</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Chile declares war on Peru and Bolivia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Chile's declaration opened the War of the Pacific, fought over the nitrate-rich Atacama Desert. Victory gave Chile the coastal provinces and left Bolivia without access to the sea.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1955</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Winston Churchill resigns as Prime Minister</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Aged 80 and in failing health, Winston Churchill stepped down as Prime Minister and was succeeded by Anthony Eden. It closed a career at the top of British politics spanning more than half a century.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-eden">Anthony Eden</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1994</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nirvana's Kurt Cobain dies in Seattle</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Kurt Cobain, singer and guitarist of Nirvana, died at his Seattle home aged 27; his body was found three days later. His death closed the band that had carried grunge into the mainstream.</p></li></ol>
